.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt LOADER.RPIBOOT 8 arm64
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm loader.rpiboot
.Nd Raspberry Pi 5 kernel loader
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the
.Fx
loader for the Raspberry Pi 5, started directly by the board's firmware
with no U-Boot and no EFI firmware in the boot path.
The firmware loads
.Pa rpiboot.bin
from its FAT boot partition as though it were a Linux arm64 kernel image,
as named by the
.Cm kernel
line of
.Pa config.txt ,
and enters it at exception level 2 with the MMU off and the physical
address of its device tree in register x0.
There is no EFI memory map; memory is what the
.Pa /memory
node of that tree lists.
The kernel is given the same device tree, with any overlays the firmware
applied.
.Pp
The interpreter is
.Xr loader_lua 8 :
.Nm
reads
.Xr loader.conf 5
and shows the boot menu.
.Ss Consoles
.Bl -tag -width "vidconsole"
.It Cm uart
The SoC PL011 on the three-pin debug connector, 115200 baud, 8 data bits,
no parity, 1 stop bit, as the firmware configured it.
Always present, and first.
.It Cm vidconsole
The HDMI display, on the framebuffer the firmware set up, when a display
was connected at power-on.
Its keyboard is a USB keyboard in HID boot protocol plugged directly into
one of the board's ports, not through a hub.
The USB controllers are in the RP1 chip behind PCIe, which
.Nm
can only reach when the firmware leaves that link up:
.Pa config.txt
must say
.Cm pciex4_reset=0 .
.El
.Ss Devices
.Bl -tag -width "disk0:"
.It Li md0:\&
A memory disk inside the loader image itself, available with no storage
driver.
The space is reserved when the loader is built
.Pq Va MD_IMAGE_SIZE , No 20 MiB by default
and a bare UFS image, such as
.Xr makefs 8
produces, is written into it afterwards.
A loader installed without one has an empty
.Li md0:\& .
.It Li disk0:\&
The SD card in the board's slot, read through the SoC's own SDHCI
controller.
MBR, GPT and BSD-label partitions are found, and UFS and FAT file systems
can be read:
.Li disk0s1:\&
is usually the firmware's FAT partition.
The card is read-only to the loader.
.El
.Pp
There is no network, no ZFS, and no driver for NVMe or USB storage.
A kernel whose root file system is on such a device is still loaded from
the card or the memory disk, and finds its root through
.Va vfs.root.mountfrom .
.Ss Choosing where to boot from
At startup
.Nm
reads
.Pa loader.env
from the first partition of the card, if it is a FAT file system.
The file holds
.Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
words separated by spaces or newlines, which become loader environment
variables.
If it sets
.Va rootdev ,
for example
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rootdev=disk0s2a:
.Ed
.Pp
and that file system has
.Pa /boot/lua/loader.lua ,
it becomes the current device: the loader's scripts,
.Pa loader.conf ,
the kernel and its modules are all read from it.
Otherwise the current device is
.Li md0:\& .
.Pp
A variable set in
.Pa loader.env
is overridden by a line for the same variable in
.Pa /boot/defaults/loader.conf
or
.Pa /boot/loader.conf ,
so settings other than
.Va rootdev
belong in
.Pa loader.conf .
.Pp
The firmware can be asked to use
.Pa tryboot.txt
in place of
.Pa config.txt
for one boot.
On such a boot
.Nm
reads
.Pa tryboot.env
before
.Pa loader.env ,
so that the trial boot can use another file system.
.Ss Memory layout
.Bl -tag -width "0x10000000-0x18000000" -compact
.It 0x00200000
.Nm
itself, with its memory disk
.It 0x04000000
device tree; set
.Cm device_tree_address
in
.Pa config.txt
so that it is clear of the loader image
.It 0x08000000
loader heap, 48 MiB
.It 0x10000000-0x18000000
kernel and modules as they are assembled
.El
.Ss Commands
In addition to the common loader commands,
.Nm
provides:
.Bl -tag -width "untryboot"
.It Ic fbinfo
Show the firmware's framebuffer.
.It Ic fbtest
Time the set-up of the framebuffer console.
.It Ic mmu
Show the loader's MMU and cache state and its memory map.
.It Ic peek
Read 32-bit device registers by physical address.
.It Ic poweroff
Power the board off, through PSCI.
.It Ic reboot
Reset the board, through PSCI.
.It Ic sdinfo
Show the SD card and its controller, or why there is no
.Li disk0:\& .
.It Ic tryboot Op Fl n
Ask the firmware to read
.Pa tryboot.txt
for the next boot only, then reset.
With
.Fl n
the request is made without resetting.
.It Ic untryboot
Withdraw that request.
.It Ic usbinfo
Show the USB controllers and what is on each port, or why there is no
keyboard.
.It Ic vcinfo
Show the firmware and board revisions, the SoC temperature, and which
configuration file this boot used.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/boot/rpiboot.bin" -compact
.It Pa /boot/rpiboot.bin
the loader as installed; copy it to the firmware's FAT partition
.El
.Pp
On the firmware's FAT partition:
.Bl -tag -width "/boot/rpiboot.bin" -compact
.It Pa config.txt
the firmware's configuration
.It Pa loader.env
loader variables read before the scripts, notably
.Va rootdev
.It Pa tryboot.env
the same, for a boot from
.Pa tryboot.txt
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The lines of
.Pa config.txt
that start the loader:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kernel=rpiboot.bin
os_check=0
device_tree_address=0x4000000
device_tree_end=0x4100000
pciex4_reset=0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr loader.conf 5 ,
.Xr loader 8 ,
.Xr loader_lua 8 ,
.Xr makefs 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
.Sh CAVEATS
Nothing controls the board's cooling fan while the loader is running.
.Pp
.Nm
does not write to the card, so features that have the loader update a
file, such as
.Xr nextboot 8 ,
do not work.
